**ESR rule for compound-index field order**
- **Equality** fields first — most selective predicates, exact match
- **Sort** fields next — index ordering must match the query's `sort()`
- **Range** fields last — narrow within the sorted slice

```javascript
// Query: active orders for a user, newest first, price under 500
db.orders.find({
  userId: "u9", status: "ACTIVE", total: { $lt: 500 }
}).sort({ createdAt: -1 });

// ESR-compliant compound index
db.orders.createIndex(
  { userId: 1, status: 1, createdAt: -1, total: 1 },
  { name: "orders_esr" }
);
```

**Left-prefix rule**
- An index `{ a: 1, b: 1, c: 1 }` serves queries on `{a}`, `{a,b}`, `{a,b,c}` — NOT on `{b}` or `{c}` alone
- Design the compound so the prefix matches your most common access paths

**Sparse — index only documents where the field exists**

```javascript
db.users.createIndex({ phone: 1 }, { sparse: true });
// Users with no phone field are skipped; smaller index

// Sparse + unique — allows many docs without the field,
// but any duplicate value among those with it is rejected
db.users.createIndex(
  { referralCode: 1 },
  { sparse: true, unique: true }
);
```

**Partial — index only documents matching a filter (stricter and more flexible)**

```javascript
db.orders.createIndex(
  { userId: 1, createdAt: -1 },
  {
    name: "orders_active_idx",
    partialFilterExpression: { status: { $in: ["ACTIVE", "PENDING"] } }
  }
);

// Index includes only documents with status ACTIVE or PENDING
// Query must include the same filter predicate to use the index:
db.orders.find({ userId: "u9", status: "ACTIVE" }).sort({ createdAt: -1 });
```

**Sparse vs partial — the distinction**
- Sparse = 'field exists'
- Partial = 'documents match a filter expression'
- Partial is a superset; prefer partial for new indexes unless the sparse semantics are exactly what you want

**Auto-delete documents after a duration**

```javascript
// Delete sessions 1 hour after createdAt
db.sessions.createIndex(
  { createdAt: 1 },
  { expireAfterSeconds: 3600 }
);

// Or target an explicit expiry timestamp
db.sessions.createIndex(
  { expiresAt: 1 },
  { expireAfterSeconds: 0 }   // delete when expiresAt < now()
);
```

**Operational notes**
- TTL monitor runs about every 60 seconds — expiry is approximate, not real-time
- TTL field must be a BSON date (or an array of dates; deletion happens when the earliest expires)
- Combining TTL with compound indexes is not supported — the TTL index must be single-field
- Use for sessions, OTPs, rate-limit windows, expirable caches, debug logs

**Multikey on array fields — one array per compound index**

```javascript
// { tags: ["java", "spring"] } — multikey index is automatic
db.posts.createIndex({ tags: 1 });

// A compound index can include only ONE array field:
db.posts.createIndex({ tags: 1, createdAt: -1 });  // ok
// db.posts.createIndex({ tags: 1, authors: 1 });   // ERROR if both are arrays
```

**Hashed for sharding**

```javascript
db.adminCommand({ shardCollection: "mydb.orders", key: { _id: "hashed" } });
```

**Spring Data MongoDB declarations**

```java
@Document(collection = "sessions")
@CompoundIndexes({
  @CompoundIndex(name = "user_status_idx",
                 def  = "{'userId': 1, 'status': 1}")
})
public class Session {
  @Id String id;
  String userId;
  String status;

  @Indexed(sparse = true)
  String referralCode;

  @Indexed(expireAfterSeconds = 3600)
  Instant expiresAt;
}

// Programmatic partial index
mongoTemplate.indexOps(Order.class).ensureIndex(
  new Index().on("userId", ASC).on("createdAt", DESC)
    .partial(PartialIndexFilter.of(
        Criteria.where("status").in("ACTIVE", "PENDING"))));
```

**Production tip** — prefer explicit migration scripts (Mongock, Liquibase for Mongo) over annotation-driven creation so index changes are reviewable in PRs.

**Goal query**

```javascript
// Find active orders for a user; show only userId, createdAt, total
db.orders.find(
  { userId: "u9", status: "ACTIVE" },
  { _id: 0, userId: 1, createdAt: 1, total: 1 }
).sort({ createdAt: -1 });
```

**Index that covers it (ESR order: Equality → Sort → Range)**

```javascript
db.orders.createIndex(
  { userId: 1, status: 1, createdAt: -1, total: 1 },
  { name: "orders_covered" }
);
```

**Key rules applied**
- `userId` and `status` are equality predicates → first in index
- `createdAt` matches the `sort()` direction → next
- `total` is included so projection is covered → last
- Projection excludes `_id` because `_id` is not in the index

**Run explain**

```javascript
db.orders.find(
  { userId: "u9", status: "ACTIVE" },
  { _id: 0, userId: 1, createdAt: 1, total: 1 }
).sort({ createdAt: -1 }).explain("executionStats");
```

**What a covered plan looks like (trimmed)**

```javascript
{
  queryPlanner: {
    winningPlan: {
      stage: "PROJECTION_COVERED",
      transformBy: { _id: 0, userId: 1, createdAt: 1, total: 1 },
      inputStage: {
        stage: "IXSCAN",
        indexName: "orders_covered",
        indexBounds: {
          userId:    ["[\"u9\", \"u9\"]"],
          status:    ["[\"ACTIVE\", \"ACTIVE\"]"],
          createdAt: ["[MaxKey, MinKey]"],
          total:     ["[MinKey, MaxKey]"]
        }
      }
    }
  },
  executionStats: {
    nReturned:           42,
    totalKeysExamined:   42,
    totalDocsExamined:    0,   // zero documents fetched — covered!
    executionTimeMillis:  1
  }
}
```

**Signals of coverage**
- `PROJECTION_COVERED` stage wraps `IXSCAN`
- **No `FETCH` stage** in the plan tree
- `totalDocsExamined: 0`
- `totalKeysExamined ≈ nReturned` — little or no index-to-result amplification

**Create a text index and query**

```javascript
// One text index per collection; can span multiple string fields
db.products.createIndex(
  { name: "text", description: "text" },
  { weights: { name: 5, description: 1 }, default_language: "english" }
);

// Query with $text
db.products.find(
  { $text: { $search: "wireless headphones" } },
  { score: { $meta: "textScore" } }
).sort({ score: { $meta: "textScore" } }).limit(20);
```

**What you get**
- Tokenization, simple stemming (limited languages), stop words
- Phrase search with quotes — `$search: "\"wireless headphones\""`
- Exclusion with minus — `$search: "wireless -wired"`
- One text index per collection (hard limit)

**What you don't get**
- Fuzzy / typo-tolerant matching
- Autocomplete / edge n-gram analyzers
- Relevance tuning beyond per-field weights
- Multi-language per document
- Faceting, highlighting, synonyms, vector search

**Define a search index (JSON spec in Atlas)**

```javascript
db.products.createSearchIndex({
  name: "default",
  definition: {
    mappings: {
      dynamic: false,
      fields: {
        name: {
          type: "string",
          analyzer: "lucene.english",
          multi: { autocomplete: { type: "autocomplete", tokenization: "edgeGram" } }
        },
        description: { type: "string", analyzer: "lucene.english" },
        category:    { type: "token" },
        embedding:   { type: "knnVector", dimensions: 1536, similarity: "cosine" }
      }
    }
  }
});
```

**Query with `$search` aggregation stage**

```javascript
db.products.aggregate([
  { $search: {
      index: "default",
      compound: {
        must: [
          { text: { query: "wireless headphones", path: ["name", "description"], fuzzy: { maxEdits: 1 } } }
        ],
        filter: [ { equals: { path: "category", value: "audio" } } ]
      },
      highlight: { path: ["name", "description"] }
  }},
  { $limit: 20 },
  { $project: {
      name: 1,
      score:      { $meta: "searchScore" },
      highlights: { $meta: "searchHighlights" }
  }}
]);

// Vector search for RAG / semantic retrieval
db.products.aggregate([
  { $vectorSearch: {
      index: "default", path: "embedding",
      queryVector: embedding, numCandidates: 200, limit: 20
  }}
]);
```
